<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umm an-Nar culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2600–2000 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Umm an-Nar culture was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, dated about 2600–2000 BCE and named after an island off Abu Dhabi. Its people lived in settlements with round stone towers, buried their dead in large round collective tombs, and mined and traded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> that Mesopotamian texts associate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a>.</p></div>
